      ":question/id": "459e46c9-4184-5656-bb8b-3280b2551168",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3x^{2}-(3)y?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,0)$,$(-1,-1)$,$(-1,3)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,0)}=3(-2)^{2}-3(0)=12, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-1)}=3(-1)^{2}-3(-1)=6, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,3)}=3(-1)^{2}-3(3)=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](images/b9/b91833eba8558d82b86f66bbc5889f2a6bb2187178170846c12238a3027cd2a0.png)"
