      ":question/id": "bcfa9b0d-38a3-51ff-9ca0-b824a54272e1",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{(-2)x+(2)y}{3}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(2,3)$,$(-1,2)$,$(-2,3)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,3)}=\\frac{(-2)(2)+(2)(3)}{3}=\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,2)}=\\frac{(-2)(-1)+(2)(2)}{3}=2, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,3)}=\\frac{(-2)(-2)+(2)(3)}{3}=\\frac{10}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](images/f3/f3e9db617e68f21ff03b3d5bf57764da9d6a0ddd81f18f718cc2c47b60c35eac.png)"
